Just a note:
     While the Saberhagan Swords trilogy is fantastic, the 
Swords were made to provide interplay between powerful, 
intelligent factions, often with armies and Gods backing 
them.  I don't think they'd fit in well in a game where 
there's only one player with any sort of guiding 
intelligence, and everyone in the game except the Wizard 
is intended to die rather quickly once encountered.  
In addition, many of the Swords required incredible 
sacrifice, which simply couldn't be born by one player.  
For instance, there is simply no possible parallel to the 
scene in which one of the more powerful evil characters in 
Saberhagan's books held Mindsword, vanquished all armies 
that came before him (another more benign character was 
forced to kill off all his own men using Shieldbreaker, 
which was before then regarded as the most powerful 
sword), only to be completely and utterly defeated by a 
woman holding Soulcutter.  She managed to survive, but was 
completely drained of ambition and lust for life-- 
permanently.  As I recall, most of those present on the 
battlefield weren't so lucky.
     There is no parallel, similarly, to the scene in 
which a god is defeated while wielding Shieldbreaker by 
unarmed men-- he wasn't able to put away the sword until 
all his opponents were defeated.
     Especially with regards to Farslayer, the Swords were 
all designed to do tremendous things to upset the balance 
of things at great and often fatal cost to the wielder.  
Some of them weren't like that-- Townsaver, Stonecutter, 
for example, but they weren't especially miraculous 
either and aren't likely to add much to the game.
     While they make great fantasy books, I'm not sure if 
they could be used well in Nethack.  Too macroscopically 
oriented.
